               <msContents>
                  <msItem class="#historica" n="1" xml:id="MS_Canon_Misc_349-item1">
                     <title key="work_12393" type="desc">Sylloge of inscriptions in Italy, with additional epitaphs, etc. ('Codex Olivae')</title> <note>Described by Mommsen as an alphabetical arrangement of inscriptions 
                        mostly derived from the collections of <persName key="person_34846891">Giovanni Marcanova</persName>, supplemented from printed books 
                     (Pontanus <title>De aspiratione</title>, 1481, and Hermolaus Barbarus <title>Castigationes Plinianae</title>, 1492), with a few unique additions (<ref target="https://objects.auxiliary.idai.world/Tei-Viewer/cgi-bin/teiviewer.php?manifest=BOOK-ZID1318078">CIL IX, p. xxxv</ref>); see also Rossi, <title>Inscriptiones Christianae urbis Romae septimo saeculo antiquiores</title>, II.i.405</note>
                     <note>The apparent colophon 'Anconius de Oliva fecit anno 1450 dies 20 Jan.' has been described as a 'falsification': Wilfred Naar and Jean-Pierre Callu, 'Furore ex zelotypia (Sur une épitaphe humaniste interprétée par B. Arnigio)', <title>Journal des Savants</title> (2009) 1  pp. 103-152, p. 109 n. 21</note>
                    
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <physDesc>
                  <objectDesc form="codex">
                     <supportDesc material="chart">
                        <support>paper</support>
                        <extent>92 leaves; small quarto</extent>
                     </supportDesc>
                  </objectDesc>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <binding notAfter="1525" notBefore="1500">
                        <p>16th century, early, <placeName key="place_1000080">Italian</placeName>, ?<placeName key="place_7008546">Genoa</placeName> (MS. after 1493, Italian): thin pasteboards, flush with text-block; dark brown leather, blind-tooled with triple lines, large dots, and a panel of the Annunciation on each cover showing Gabriel, Mary and two smaller figures amidst a flowering plant set in a pot above initals M B, all surrounded by the Ave Maria in capitals; traces of four sets of lost ties; spine-linings from a devotional manuscript, 15th century, Italian. Added endleaves of carta bassanese, 18th century, Italian. <dimensions type="binding" unit="mm">
                              <height precision="medium" quantity="243">c. 243</height>
                              <width max="172" min="170">170–172</width>
                              <depth precision="medium" quantity="25"> c. 25</depth>
                           </dimensions> (book closed).</p>
                     </binding>
                  </bindingDesc>
